#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:0
msgid ""
"Learn how TPM-backed disk encryption protects your data on Ubuntu Desktop, "
"and how the recovery key, PIN and passphrase work."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:8
msgid "Topics: Installation Security"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:12
msgid "Hardware-backed disk encryption"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:14
msgid ""
"Hardware-backed disk encryption is a convenient way to keep your data "
"secure. It automatically decrypts the data on your disk at startup, while "
"keeping your data encrypted at rest. This eliminates the need to enter a "
"passphrase every time you start up your machine: you just need to enter your "
"user password to log in. Optionally, you can set a disk encryption PIN or "
"passphrase for additional security."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:17
msgid "How hardware-backed disk encryption works"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:19
msgid ""
"When you enable hardware-backed disk encryption, the encryption keys for "
"your disk are automatically generated and stored safely in your computer’s "
"Trusted Platform Module (TPM)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:21
msgid ""
"At every startup, the TPM verifies that your computer's hardware and "
"critical boot software have not been altered. If the TPM detects any "
"unauthorized changes, it refuses to unlock the disk, unless the user reverts "
"the changes or provides a recovery key. This means that your data is "
"protected even if your device is stolen: the attacker can't unlock your disk "
"if they remove the disk and install it in another computer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:23
msgid ""
"In other words, anyone who wants to access your data must know your user "
"password. This provides more security than an unencrypted Ubuntu "
"installation, where the attacker can just remove your disk and read your "
"data, or start another system on your computer. This feature is also more "
"convenient than traditional disk encryption, such as LUKS: you only have to "
"remember your user password and there's no additional disk password."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:25
msgid ""
"For technical details, see {external:doc}`security-features/storage/"
"encryption-full-disk` in the Ubuntu security documentation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:29
msgid "Recovery key"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:31
msgid ""
"A recovery key is a long string of numbers that you can use to recover the "
"data on your encrypted disk. You receive the recovery key at the end of the "
"Ubuntu installation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:33
msgid ""
"You should store it somewhere safe outside of your computer, such as in a "
"cloud-based password manager."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:35
msgid ""
"The recovery key is stored securely. You can't retrieve it but you can reset "
"it in the Security Center to get a new one. However, you must be able to log "
"into your Ubuntu session before you can reset the recovery key."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:38
msgid ""
"If you lose your recovery key, you might lose access to your data in certain "
"scenarios. While you're logged in, replace the existing recovery key as soon "
"as possible. See {ref}`tpm-fde-get-a-new-recovery-key`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:42
msgid "When Ubuntu asks for your recovery key"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:44
msgid "You need the recovery key in several different situations:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:46
msgid ""
"If you **move your disk** to a new computer, you need to enter your recovery "
"key before your new computer can access the data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:48
msgid ""
"If you **forget your disk PIN or passphrase**, Ubuntu asks for your recovery "
"key to unlock the disk."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:50
msgid ""
"If you **change the hardware, firmware** or other components of your "
"computer, you need to enter your recovery key to confirm that you trust the "
"new configuration. This includes the following changes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:52
msgid "Changes to hardware components in your computer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:53
msgid "Updates to BIOS, UEFI and firmware"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:54
msgid "Changes to boot settings, such as boot order or Secure Boot"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:55
msgid ""
"Errors with authentication, such as entering a wrong password too many times"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:56
msgid "Changes to certain settings, such as organization security policies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:57
msgid "Resetting or clearing of the TPM module"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:59
msgid ""
"If you're planning to do any of these changes on your computer, make sure "
"that you know your recovery key first."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:62
msgid ""
"If the system asks you for your recovery key even when no hardware or "
"software on your computer has changed, it might be an attack that tries to "
"gain access to your data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:66
msgid "Ubuntu alongside another encrypted system"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:68
msgid ""
"If you have additional encrypted drives or another encrypted operating "
"system on your computer, make sure to safely store the recovery keys to all "
"your drives."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:70
msgid ""
"For example, if you install Ubuntu with hardware-backed disk encryption "
"alongside Microsoft Windows with BitLocker enabled, you need to store both "
"recovery keys for Ubuntu and Windows."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:72
msgid ""
"You might need a recovery key per each drive in case of the events listed in "
"{ref}`tpm-fde-when-ubuntu-asks-for-your-recovery-key`. Updating firmware "
"with the Firmware Updater in Ubuntu might require you to provide recovery "
"keys for non-Ubuntu drives, too."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:74
msgid "Windows BitLocker"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:76
msgid ""
"If you use BitLocker on Windows or have an encrypted Windows installation in "
"your machine, see the Microsoft documentation on how to [back up](https://"
"support.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/back-up-your-bitlocker-recovery-key-"
"e63607b4-77fb-4ad3-8022-d6dc428fbd0d) and [find](https://"
"support.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/find-your-bitlocker-recovery-"
"key-6b71ad27-0b89-ea08-f143-056f5ab347d6) recovery keys."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:78
msgid ""
"If you need to apply changes to your system, you can also disable BitLocker "
"temporarily and re-enable BitLocker after the changes have been made. See "
"{ref}`turn-off-bitlocker-in-windows` and {ref}`bitlocker-during-ubuntu-"
"installation`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:80
msgid "Other platforms"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:82
msgid ""
"When using recovery keys for other platforms, see the relevant vendor’s "
"documentation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:86
msgid "Encryption PIN and passphrase"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:88
msgid ""
"Optionally, you can set a PIN or passphrase. You enter them every time your "
"computer starts. The PIN is a numeric code while the passphrase is an "
"alphanumerical password."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:90
msgid ""
"You can enable, change and disable the PIN or passphrase in the Security "
"Center app."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:92
msgid ""
"If your system doesn't provide a hardware root of trust, the Ubuntu "
"installer requires that you set a PIN or passphrase to ensure a level of "
"security. You can disable it after installation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:94
msgid "PIN and passphrase protection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:96
msgid ""
"Both the PIN and passphrase can provide additional security, depending on "
"your use case:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:98
msgid ""
"In a **server** environment, it's more likely that somebody steals your disk "
"rather than the whole computer. Therefore, you might prefer encryption "
"without a PIN or passphrase, which ensures disk security and doesn't require "
"physical access to reboot the server."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:100
msgid ""
"With a **laptop**, it's more likely that somebody steals your whole "
"computer, for example when traveling. Therefore, you might want to add the "
"disk encryption PIN or passphrase so that your data is protected even before "
"the attacker can break through your login screen."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:102
msgid ""
"With a **desktop** computer, consider which of the risk factors is more "
"likely."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:104
msgid "Additional passphrase protection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:106
msgid ""
"Compared to the PIN, the passphrase adds protection against attacks "
"targeting your TPM chip."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:108
msgid ""
"With the passphrase, your disk is encrypted both by the automatically-"
"generated encryption keys, stored in your TPM, and by your passphrase. As a "
"result, your passphrase is still needed to decrypt your disk even if the TPM "
"gets compromised. For instance, the passphrase protects you against a "
"malicious firmware update from the TPM manufacturer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:110
msgid "Enabling the passphrase is particularly useful in the following cases:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:112
msgid "In security-conscious contexts"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:113
msgid "For users that handle sensitive data"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:114
msgid "For users that might be subject to targeted attacks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:117
msgid "Enable hardware-backed disk encryption"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:119
msgid ""
"To protect your Ubuntu installation with hardware-backed disk encryption, "
"see {ref}`encrypt-your-disk-with-tpm`."
msgstr ""
